 # Regularization and Loss Functions
 
+Also called: Augmented Loss, Regularization Terms, Auxiliary Losses
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization and Loss Functions define the objective landscape for optimizing Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), extending the standard photometric reconstruction loss to address geometric ambiguity, overfitting, and semantic inconsistency, particularly in sparse-view or ill-posed scenarios [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. Unlike standard photometric optimization which minimizes pixel-wise color differences, this family incorporates geometric priors, structural similarity, semantic embeddings, and generative diffusion models to constrain the density and color fields [S1, 7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md, 8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *what* to optimize (the loss terms) rather than the *how* of sampling, and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by providing the mathematical definitions of the error metrics rather than hyperparameter schedules.
+Regularization and Loss Functions extend the standard photometric reconstruction loss ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}}$) with auxiliary terms ($\mathcal{L}_k$) to constrain the optimization of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs), particularly in scenarios with sparse views, ambiguous geometry, or generative synthesis [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]. The total loss is defined as $\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}} + \sum_k \lambda_k \mathcal{L}_k$ [S1, 10636-specific-loss-formulations.md]. This family sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by focusing on the *objective function* rather than the sampling strategy, and from [Training Configuration](2332-training-configuration.md) by defining the specific mathematical constraints rather than hyperparameter schedules.
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate within an augmented loss structure where the total loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}$ is a weighted sum of a photometric reconstruction term and one or more regularization terms $\mathcal{L}_k$ [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md]:
+All methods in this family operate within the differentiable volume rendering framework where gradients flow from the loss $\mathcal{L}$ back through the rendered color $\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{r})$ and depth $\hat{\mathbf{D}}(\mathbf{r})$ to the network parameters $\theta$ (density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$) [S1, 7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md, 8582-regnerf.md].
 
-$$ \mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}} + \sum_k \lambda_k \mathcal{L}_k $$
+The rendering process computes expected color and depth along rays $r(t) = o + td$ using sample points $t_i$ with weights $w_i = T(t_i)\rho(t_i)$, where $\rho(t_i) = 1 - \exp(-\sigma(\mathbf{r}(t_i))(t_{i+1} - t_i))$ and $T(t_i) = \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \rho(t_j))$ [S1]. The rendering weights $w_i$ serve as the probability distribution of absorption along the ray, often denoted as $h(t) = T(t)\sigma(t)$, which is used in distributional geometric losses [S1, S2].
 
-The photometric loss $\mathcal{L}_{\text{photo}}$ is typically the Mean Squared Error (MSE) or L1 loss between the rendered pixel color $\hat{\mathbf{C}}(\mathbf{r})$ and the ground-truth color $\mathbf{C}(\mathbf{r})$ for a ray $\mathbf{r}$ [S1, 10895-s3im-stochastic-structural-similarity.md]. The rendering process computes expected color $\mathbf{C}(r)$ and depth $\mathbf{D}(r)$ along rays $r(t) = o + td$ using sample points $t_i$ with weights $w_i = T(t_i)\rho(t_i)$, where $\rho(t_i) = 1 - \exp(-\sigma(\mathbf{r}(t_i))(t_{i+1} - t_i))$ and $T(t_i) = \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \rho(t_j))$ [S1].
+The rendered depth is typically the expected distance along the ray:
+$$ \hat{D}(\mathbf{r}) = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i t_i $$
+where $w_i = T_i (1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i))$ are the rendering weights, $t_i$ are sample depths, and $T_i$ is the accumulated transmittance [S1, S2].
 
