 ## Background
-The concept of regularization in NeRF optimization emerged as a response to the inherent ambiguities in inverse volume rendering, particularly when training data is sparse or noisy. Early NeRF implementations relied solely on photometric MSE loss, which often resulted in "floaters" (spurious geometry) and blurry reconstructions in unobserved regions [S1, 7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md].
-
-RegNeRF [8582-regnerf.md] was among the first to explicitly introduce depth smoothness and color priors via Normalizing Flows to stabilize training on sparse views [S1]. Subsequently, geometric supervision methods [8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md] incorporated external depth priors (e.g., from monocular estimators) to constrain the density field [S1, S2]. The integration of semantic information via CLIP embeddings [11955-semantic-consistency-loss-clip-based.md] addressed photometric ambiguity by enforcing high-level structural consistency [S3]. More recently, diffusion models have been leveraged as powerful generative priors, with Diffusion-regularized NeRF [7381-diffusion-regularized-nerf.md] backpropagating score-based gradients to enforce realistic RGBD distributions [S1, S2]. Structural similarity metrics like S3IM [10895-s3im-stochastic-structural-similarity.md] were adapted to capture non-local correlations that pixel-wise losses miss [S1]. Confidence-based calibration [11347-confidence-based-loss-calibration.md] addressed the instability of joint pose and field optimization [S1].
-
-These methods are widely used in novel view synthesis, 3D generation from text/images (e.g., DreamGaussian, Magic3D), and dynamic scene reconstruction [S1, S2, S6].
+The use of regularization in NeRF optimization emerged as a response to the ill-posed nature of the inverse rendering problem, particularly when training data is sparse or noisy. Early works like RegNeRF [S1] introduced geometric and color priors to stabilize training from sparse views. Subsequent research expanded this to include semantic consistency [S1, S3], diffusion-based priors [S1, S2], and specific loss formulations for dynamic scenes [S1] or physically-based rendering [S3]. The family continues to evolve with methods addressing specific artifacts like floaters [S4], occlusions [S1], and color saturation in generative tasks [S6].
 
